// Error recovery in full, over an input where the parse both fails and gets going again. `@error ";"` says that a
// statement which went wrong may be resumed at the next semicolon, so the recovery pops the states of the half parsed
// statement, shifts the error symbol in the state which allows it, and carries on.
//
// The order of the events is the whole content of this case, and it is report, discard, pop, resume. The token which
// caused the error is kept rather than thrown away, because the state the recovery resumes in is usually waiting for
// exactly it; only a parse which fails again on that very same token discards it. Every round reports the states it
// popped, even when it popped none, so the number of events per round does not depend on how far the stack unwound.
//
// The statements around the broken one are there to show that the parse really continues: the trace has to contain the
// complete parse of the third statement after the recovery, and the shift of the end of input symbol and the accept
// after that.
//
// The scanner is not what this case is about, so its rules are single characters and runs of digits.

@scanner {
    WHITESPACE: /[ \t\n]+/ @skip;

    NUMBER:    /[0-9]+/;
    PLUS:      "+";
    SEMICOLON: ";";
}

@parser {
    statements
        : statements statement
        | statement
        ;

    statement
        : expression ";"
        | @error ";"
        ;

    expression
        : NUMBER
        | expression "+" NUMBER
        ;
}
